88:          <p>The International Energy Agency's March 19 contributions page says the agreement announced on 11 March makes "400 million barrels of oil available to the market." It says the overall release "will largely consist of crude oil, while in Europe the contributions will primarily take the form of refined oil products." It calls the action "the sixth time" members have acted together since the agency "was created in 1974," after actions "in 1991, 2005, 2011, and twice in 2022," and calls the Middle East war "the largest supply disruption in the history of the global oil market." Executive Director Fatih Birol's remarks are named on that page.<Cite s="iea-contributions-2026-03-19" passage="400 million barrels of oil available to the market" /><Cite s="iea-contributions-2026-03-19" passage="This is the sixth time that IEA Member countries have taken emergency collective action" /><Cite s="iea-contributions-2026-03-19" passage="Previous collective actions were taken in 1991, 2005, 2011, and twice in 2022." /><Cite s="iea-contributions-workbook-2026-03-19" passage="Total IEA,426.03075080054771" /></p>
92:          <DataTable columns={["Record", "Wording of the 100 million barrels"]} rows={statementRows} />
102:              <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="3">President Donald Trump posted that Europe has just agreed to release a massive amount of their heavily stocked Diesel Oil and that the process will begin immediately. The joint statement gives no number for the diesel, names no country that will supply it, and records no barrel delivered; it calls the diesel release "substantial" and places it in the first 20 days.<Cite s="trumpstruth-post-2026-10-02" passage="Europe has just agreed to release a massive amount of their heavily stocked Diesel Oil. The process will begin immediately." /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="taking into account commitments that have already been fulfilled, we will implement our commitments with a coordinated release through the IEA of 100 million barrels (MB) to begin immediately over 4 months, including a frontloaded substantial diesel release within the first 20 days" /></p>
108:              <h3>100 million barrels of diesel in 20 days, or 50 and 50</h3>
109:              <p data-layer="narrative">Reuters reported a demand to "release 100 million barrels of diesel within a 20-day window". A Friday, October 2, proposal, before the statement, said European countries would release 50 million barrels of diesel and International Energy Agency members would release 50 million barrels of crude oil.<Cite s="reuters-eu-proposal-2026-10-02" passage="release 100 million barrels of diesel within a 20-day window" /><Cite s="reuters-eu-proposal-2026-10-02" passage="European countries would release 50 million barrels of diesel and International Energy Agency members would release 50 million barrels of crude oil" /></p>
110:              <p data-layer="narrative">The day before, Reuters reported: "A second source, based in a European capital, said that the US has asked the EU to release 120 million barrels of diesel in the next six months."<Cite s="reuters-diesel-demand-2026-10-01" passage="A second source, based in a European capital, said that the US has asked the EU to release 120 million barrels of diesel in the next six months." /></p>
114:              <h3>100 million barrels of crude oil and fuel</h3>
115:              <p data-layer="narrative">The Wall Street Journal's lede said the countries would "release 100 million barrels of crude oil and fuel from their emergency stocks". IEA Executive Director Fatih Birol noted that around {marchSoFar.barrels} million barrels of the IEA collective action announced on 11 March have so far been released, representing over {marchSoFar.share} of the {release.marchTotal} barrels originally pledged in the action.<Cite s="wsj-g7-release-2026-10-02" passage="release 100 million barrels of crude oil and fuel from their emergency stocks" /><Cite s="iea-g7-meeting-2026-10-02" passage="He noted that around 325 million barrels of the IEA collective action announced on 11 March have so far been released, representing over 80% of the 400 million barrels originally pledged in the action." /></p>
119:        <SourcedBlock source="trumpstruth-post-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
130:        <p data-layer="narrative">The March 11 G7 presidency communique welcomed a release of "de libérer jusqu’à 400 millions de barils de réserves stratégiques", to release up to 400 million barrels of strategic reserves.<Cite s="elysee-g7-communique-2026-03-11" passage="de libérer jusqu’à 400 millions de barils de réserves stratégiques" /></p>
136:        <p data-layer="narrative">U.S. stocks of distillate fuel oil were 105,180 thousand barrels in the week of September 25, against 103,391 thousand in the week of August 21.<Cite s="eia-distillate-2026-10-03" passage="Stocks of Distillate Fuel Oil" /><Cite s="eia-distillate-2026-10-03" passage="(Thousand Barrels)" /><Cite s="eia-distillate-2026-10-03" passage="08/21/26 08/28/26 09/04/26 09/11/26 09/18/26 09/25/26 View History U.S. 103,391 104,187 106,274 107,859 107,431 105,180" /></p>
