23:G7 release only a 'short-term' measure - professor
24:A Professor of Sustainability at University College Cork has described the G7's release of diesel and oil reserves as a "short-term" measure, adding that there will be a period of high oil prices across the globe.
25:Speaking on RTÉ’s Six One, Prof Brian Ó Gallachóir said the only way to alleviate the supply shock in the medium term is for the US and Israel to cease their attacks on Iran.
26:"It's really a short-term measure. What they've agreed is to release 100m barrels of strategic reserves.
27:"That will be over a four-week period and there'll be a particular front loading of diesel over the next 20 days. So, it is significant," Prof Ó Gallachóir said.
28:He added: "Just to put that quantity into context, at the moment, the blockade at the Strait of Hormuz is blocking between 15-20m barrels every day in terms of really impacting and restricting the supply of oil.
29:"And that's what's causing these significant oil prices.
30:"So, to dampen down the prices in a longer-term way, I mean, essentially the US and Israel need to stop the attacks on Iran and enable the opening up of the Strait of Hormuz."
31:We need your consent to load this rte-player contentWe use rte-player to manage extra content that can set cookies on your device and collect data about your activity. Please review their details and accept them to load the content.Manage Preferences
32:Prof Ó Gallachóir said Ireland should reduce its dependence on oil, adding that it is "crippling us in terms of the cost of living crisis".
33:"There's going to be so much of the budget taken up with measures to dampen down the effects of what's happening because of what's happening geopolitically, but it's linked to our dependence on oil.
34:"So, if we wean ourselves off oil, it puts us in a much stronger position to be able to plan more rationally going forward in terms of our energy costs," he said.
35:However, Prof Ó Gallachóir said any short-term reduction in the price of oil will be tempered by damage to refineries in the Gulf from Iranian strikes during the war.
36:"Initially it was the flow of oil was what was blocked. But then with the attacks on both sides, we had attacks on infrastructure.
37:"So, the ability to refine the oil, to turn the crude oil into the things that we use, petrol, diesel and kerosene. Those things will really take time to recover.
38:"This short-term blip in terms of releasing the strategic reserves will certainly dampen down prices in the short term.
39:"But we are facing into a period of high oil prices and weaning ourselves off oil is really the only long-term strategy that we can pursue."
